WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.989
What Is Instructional Design?.

2
00:00:02.989 --> 00:00:09.265
ID 553 - Module 1: Introduction to Instructional Design
Models.

3
00:00:09.265 --> 00:00:15.242
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.842 --> 00:00:18.083
Let's start with a definition.

5
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:31.457
Instructional design is the systematic process of
translating principles of learning and instruction into
plans for instructional materials, activities, resources,
and evaluation.

6
00:00:31.457 --> 00:00:33.624
Three words carry the weight.

7
00:00:33.624 --> 00:00:40.498
Systematic: the work proceeds through identifiable phases
whose outputs feed the next phase.

8
00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:44.458
It's not a recipe, but it isn't improvisation either.

9
00:00:44.458 --> 00:00:49.987
Principles of learning: the work rests on evidence about how
people learn.

10
00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:54.918
A designer who ignores that evidence is decorating, not
designing.

11
00:00:54.918 --> 00:01:01.418
And plans: the product is something another person can
implement, evaluate, and revise.

12
00:01:02.018 --> 00:01:03.806
The field has three roots.

13
00:01:03.806 --> 00:01:06.970
The first is military and industrial training.

14
00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:17.628
During the Second World War, psychologists including Robert
Gagne were asked to train enormous numbers of people quickly
and reliably on complex equipment.

15
00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:26.567
They developed task analysis, criterion-referenced testing,
and the practice of specifying objectives before building
instruction.

16
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:38.601
The systems approach that emerged, treating instruction as a
set of interacting components engineered toward a measurable
outcome, is the ancestor of every model you'll study.

17
00:01:39.201 --> 00:01:41.260
The second root is psychology.

18
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:48.120
Skinner's programmed instruction contributed small steps,
active responding, and immediate feedback.

19
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.060
Mager gave designers a format for writing measurable
objectives in 1962.

20
00:01:53.060 --> 00:02:07.331
Gagne's Conditions of Learning argued that different types
of outcomes require different instructional conditions, and
his nine events of instruction became the most widely used
lesson framework in the field.

21
00:02:07.331 --> 00:02:20.503
Cognitive psychology later reframed learning as the
construction of mental models and drew attention to the
limits of working memory, the power of prior knowledge, and
the problem of transfer.

22
00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:25.066
The third root is constructivism and the learning sciences.

23
00:02:25.066 --> 00:02:42.798
Constructivist critics argued that the systems approach
could produce sterile, decontextualized instruction, and
pushed the field toward authentic tasks, collaboration, and
reflection through situated learning, problem-based
learning, and cognitive apprenticeship.

24
00:02:42.798 --> 00:02:49.380
The learning sciences added design-based research and a
closer partnership with cognitive science.

25
00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:57.036
Contemporary instructional design draws on all three roots
and argues, productively, about the balance among them.

26
00:02:57.636 --> 00:03:02.741
The most durable idea in the field is that instruction is a
system.

27
00:03:02.741 --> 00:03:11.731
Learners, content, methods, media, environment, and
assessment interact, and their interactions determine the
outcome.

28
00:03:11.731 --> 00:03:14.931
Change one component and the others shift.

29
00:03:14.931 --> 00:03:18.436
Add a video and you change the cognitive load.

30
00:03:18.436 --> 00:03:22.703
Move a course online and you change the social presence.

31
00:03:22.703 --> 00:03:26.208
Shorten the practice and you change retention.

32
00:03:26.208 --> 00:03:28.036
Two consequences follow.

33
00:03:28.036 --> 00:03:34.893
Analysis comes before design, because you cannot engineer a
system you have not described.

34
00:03:34.893 --> 00:03:42.284
And evaluation is part of the system, not an afterthought;
every model closes the loop with data.

35
00:03:42.884 --> 00:03:46.651
What does a designer actually do on a project?

36
00:03:46.651 --> 00:03:54.268
First, a needs assessment: is there a performance gap, and
is instruction the right solution?

37
00:03:54.268 --> 00:03:55.497
Often it isn't.

38
00:03:55.497 --> 00:04:07.372
A job aid, a process change, or a better incentive may fix
the problem faster, and designers who skip this question
build training nobody needed.

39
00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:11.877
Then front-end analysis of learners, context, and task.

40
00:04:11.877 --> 00:04:17.610
Then design: objectives, assessments, sequence, strategies,
and media.

41
00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.624
Then development, implementation, and evaluation.

42
00:04:21.624 --> 00:04:28.831
And underneath all of it, project management: scope,
schedule, stakeholders, and budget.

43
00:04:29.431 --> 00:04:32.329
Here's what skipping analysis looks like.

44
00:04:32.329 --> 00:04:41.235
A hospital replaces its patient records system and retrains
four hundred nurses with the vendor's eight-hour classroom
course.

45
00:04:41.235 --> 00:04:49.435
Nobody asks what the nurses already know, when they can
actually attend, or which workflows are most likely to fail.

46
00:04:49.435 --> 00:04:58.553
The training happens six weeks before go-live, everyone
forgets it, and the help desk takes nine hundred calls in
the first week.

47
00:04:58.553 --> 00:05:02.229
You'll analyze exactly this situation in module two.

48
00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:05.410
Keep it in mind as you read about the models.

49
00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.027
One last point.

50
00:05:07.027 --> 00:05:09.606
Teachers design instruction every day.

51
00:05:09.606 --> 00:05:26.228
What distinguishes instructional design as a discipline is
the explicitness and the systems view: objectives are
written, assessments are aligned before activities are
chosen, and the design is documented so others can implement
and evaluate it.

52
00:05:26.228 --> 00:05:35.659
Many teachers find that learning instructional design makes
their tacit expertise visible, and that visibility is what
makes it improvable.

53
00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:44.480
Before the next page, write one paragraph on what would go
wrong in a project that skipped analysis and went straight
to building.

54
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.311
Bring it to the discussion.
